//~ compile_fail(backend)
//~ error[KORU030]: Phantom state mismatch

// NEGATIVE (must FAIL to compile): take the READ lock and release it as a
// WRITER. This is the headline refusal of `unikraft/lock`.
//
// `write.release` accepts only <!writing>, minted only by `write.acquire`.
// `read.acquire` mints <reading!>. The two are four characters apart at the call
// site and are not interchangeable at run time.
//
// WHAT THE C DOES INSTEAD. `uk_rwlock_wunlock` opens with
// `UK_ASSERT(rwl->nactive == -1)` (rwlock.c:120) and then sets `nactive = 0`
// unconditionally. Called while a reader holds the lock, `nactive` goes from 1
// to 0: the lock now reads as FREE while the reader is still inside it, so the
// next `uk_rwlock_wlock` finds `nactive == 0`, is admitted immediately, and
// writes concurrently with the reader.
//
// The assertion catches it — in a debug build. `UK_ASSERT` is `do {} while(0)`
// whenever `CONFIG_LIBUKDEBUG_ENABLE_ASSERT` is off, which is every image
// anyone ships. So in production this mistake produces no hang, no crash and no
// message: a data race that shows up as corrupted state somewhere else, later,
// on someone else's machine.
//
// This is the arm of the pair that fails SILENTLY. Its mirror image,
// `negative_runlock_after_wlock.kz`, at least hangs.
//
// EXPECTED: error[KORU030]: Phantom state mismatch: expected
//           'unikraft.lock:writing' but got 'unikraft.lock:reading!'
//           for argument 'lock'

// The `unikraft` namespace is declared here, not inherited: koruc's built-in
// alias probes the sibling koru-libs CHECKOUT, so in a worktree this file would
// otherwise compile against main's lifts instead of the ones beside it.
~std/compiler:paths {
    unikraft: {{ ENTRY }}/../..
}

~import unikraft/lock
~import std/io

~unikraft/lock:probe
| ready proof |> unikraft/lock:create(proof.locks)
    | lock guard |> unikraft/lock:read.acquire(lock: guard): shared
        |> unikraft/lock:write.release(lock: shared): done
            |> std/io:print.ln("should never get here")
            |> unikraft/lock:destroy(lock: done)
    | unavailable why |> std/io:print.ln("create failed: {{ why:s }}")
| unsupported why |> std/io:print.ln("uk_rwlock layout refused: {{ why:s }}")
